{
    "$schema": "vendor/infection/infection/resources/schema.json",
    "source": {
        "directories": ["src"]
    },
    "logs": {
        "text": "php://stderr",
        "summary": "build/infection-summary.log"
    },
    "testFramework": "testo",
    // Measured with --with-uncovered and zend.assertions=-1 (as in CI), with the
    // coverage-job's redis service reachable (REDIS_HOST) and ext-redis loaded:
    // 379 mutants, 18 Escaped, 7 Not Covered, 4 Ignored, overall MSI 93%.
    // The 7 Not Covered are Throw_ mutants on error paths a test cannot stage
    // (tempnam()/file_put_contents() failures in AtomicMaintenanceFileWriter,
    // the unreachable-by-construction JsonException catch in StateJson::encode,
    // and Redis SET/DEL server rejections in PhpRedisMaintenanceClient — a real
    // server accepts every SET/DEL these tests issue). All 18 survivors are
    // genuinely equivalent or unreachable:
    // - IpMatcher::isValidRule() line 49: `(int) $prefix` — $prefix already matched
    //   `\A\d+\z`, so comparing the numeric string with `<=` is the same as comparing
    //   the cast integer (an overflowing prefix becomes a float and is still > 128).
    // - IpMatcher::matchesRule() line 73: `$fullBytes > 0` -> `>= 0`: with zero full
    //   bytes both substr() calls return '' and compare equal.
    // - IpMatcher::matchesRule() line 83: `0xff` -> `0xfe` in `& 0xff`: the shifted
    //   value has a clear low bit for every reachable $remainingBits (1..7).
    // - AtomicMaintenanceFileWriter::write() line 27: tempnam()'s $prefix
    //   argument (basename + '.tmp-') only names a transient temp file that
    //   gets renamed away (success) or unlinked (failure) — never observable.
    // - DefaultMaintenanceResponseFactory::prefersJson() lines 46/47: the
    //   `trim($accept) === ''` early-return is redundant with the general
    //   loop below it — for any whitespace-only $accept, every item's media
    //   type ends up empty too, so jsonMentioned stays false and the final
    //   `return !$jsonMentioned` already yields true independently.
    // - Same method, line 56: the outer `trim($item)` before splitting on
    //   ';' is redundant — the media type is re-trimmed at line 57 and each
    //   parameter is re-trimmed at line 61, so no fragment's leading/trailing
    //   whitespace ever survives to matter.
    // - Same method, line 81 (`if ($jsonQuality > $htmlQuality) return true`):
    //   $jsonQuality and $htmlQuality are always clamped to [0, 1], so
    //   whenever this condition is true, $jsonQuality > $htmlQuality >= 0
    //   forces $jsonQuality > 0, which independently satisfies line 88's
    //   `$jsonQuality > 0.0` and returns true anyway.
    // - StateJson::decode() lines 44/48 (formerly FileMaintenanceProvider, moved
    //   unchanged): each early return guards a condition where, if bypassed, every
    //   field read via `$data[...] ?? default` on a non-array/undefined `$data`
    //   gracefully degrades to the exact same default MaintenanceState via PHP's `??`
    //   operator — verified by tracing every non-array json_decode() result
    //   type (string/int/float/bool/null) through the rest of the method.
    // - StateJson::decode() line 42 (json_decode depth 512 +/- 1): no fixture
    //   nests deeper than a handful of levels, so 511/513 behave identically.
    // - StateJson::encode() line 32 (BitwiseOr on JSON_THROW_ON_ERROR, and
    //   `: 0` -> `: 1` when not pretty): a MaintenanceState contains only
    //   bools, ints, IP strings and hex hashes — no value can ever contain a
    //   `/` or a unicode char, so removing JSON_UNESCAPED_SLASHES or forcing
    //   JSON_PRETTY_PRINT never changes the encoded document these tests see.
    // - StateJson::encode() JSON_THROW_ON_ERROR removal: encoding an array of
    //   scalars cannot fail, the catch is unreachable by construction.
    "minMsi": 92,
    "mutators": {
        "@default": true,
        // Constructor/method parameter declarations with a default value
        // (`private int $x = 100,`, `bool $strict = false,`): the mutant sits on a
        // signature line with no executable opcode under pcov, so it is reported Not
        // Covered whatever the tests do. Only a type-prefixed `$name = default,` line
        // matches; a body line cannot (no type before the variable). Do NOT add ^ or $:
        // Infection wraps the pattern as ^-\s*REGEX$ over the removed diff line.
        // Verified 2026-11-01 by diffing the survivor lists with and without it:
        // bulkhead 6, circuit-breaker 13, json-ld 19 mutants removed, all of them
        // such signature lines; Escaped and Timed Out unchanged.
        "global-ignoreSourceCodeByRegex": [
            "(?:(?:private|protected|public|readonly)\\s+)*\\??[A-Za-z_\\\\][\\w\\\\|]*\\s+\\$\\w+\\s*=\\s*[^,;]+,"
        ],
        // Off: with --with-uncovered a PublicVisibility mutant sits on a parameterless
        // method's signature line, which has no executable opcode under pcov/xdebug,
        // so it is reported Not Covered and can never be killed, whatever #[Covers]
        // says. Measured on pinned-http-client 2026-11-01: 4 of 4 such mutants were
        // Not Covered, none killed.
        "PublicVisibility": false,
        // Off for the same reason: a ProtectedVisibility mutant on a protected method's
        // signature line is Not Covered and cannot be killed. Measured on
        // understudy-phpunit 2026-11-01: 4 of 4 such mutants were Not Covered.
        "ProtectedVisibility": false
    }
}
